    "body": "# PR type\n- [x] Bug Fix\n- [ ] New Feature\n- [ ] Document Updates\n- [ ] More Models or Datasets Support\n\n# PR information\n\n`swift.utils.time_synchronize()` falls through to `torch.cuda.synchronize()` when CUDA, NPU and MUSA are unavailable. On this Mac, both the CPU call and synchronization after real MPS tensor operations raise `AssertionError: Torch not compiled with CUDA enabled`.\n\nMake CPU synchronization a no-op and use `torch.mps.synchronize()` for MPS. Honor explicitly requested CPU/MPS devices. Keep device forwarding for CUDA/NPU/MUSA, MUSA's precedence over patched CUDA, and errors for explicitly requested unavailable CUDA devices.\n\n## Experiment results\n\nmacOS arm64, Python 3.11.15, PyTorch 2.4.1:\n\n- Before: `python -m pytest tests/utils/test_synchronize.py -q` — **7 failed, 4 passed**.\n- After: `python -m pytest tests/utils/test_synchronize.py tests/utils/test_musa.py::TestMusaDeviceHelpers tests/utils/test_max_reserved_memory.py -q` — **22 passed**.\n- `pre-commit run --all-files` — all ten hooks passed.\n\nThe CPU and MPS tests ran on real devices; CUDA/NPU/MUSA routing was tested with mocks. No full model training was run.\n\nPrepared and tested with Codex assistance.\n",
